 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+Sometimes a scrape job backed by an auto-discovery source (cloud API, orchestrator, catalog, etc.) may unexpectedly
+return an abnormally big number of targets because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side - for example,
+a tag filter accidentally removed, or a catalog query becoming too broad. Scraping all of these targets at once
+can spike CPU and memory usage of `vmagent` and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent`
+instance until the misconfiguration is fixed.
+
+The `max_scrape_targets` option at the [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs)
+level protects against this by limiting the number of targets, which can be simultaneously scraped for the given
+`job_name`, regardless of which [service discovery mechanism(s)](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/)
+produced these targets:
+
+```yaml
+scrape_configs:
+- job_name: my-job
+  max_scrape_targets: 1000
+  kubernetes_sd_configs:
+  - role: pod
+```
+
+If the number of targets discovered for `my-job` exceeds `1000`, then only `1000` of them are scraped, while the rest
+are dropped and shown at the [`/targets`](#monitoring) and `/api/v1/targets` pages with the `max_scrape_targets` reason,
+similarly to targets dropped because of relabeling.
+
+By default, the number of simultaneously scraped targets per job is unlimited. The default can be changed globally
+for all the jobs via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag, and then overridden per job via the
+`max_scrape_targets` option.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
